=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router7
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — higher latency to Campus 2 services with router7 logging an OSPF neighbour going down — is real, but the team's hypothesis (ospfd crash) is wrong. ospfd on router7 is running normally with three Full OSPF adjacencies on its three other interfaces (eth2/eth4/eth5). The daemon never crashed.

The actual fault is that router7's **eth3** (IP 10.0.0.1/24, on the `link_wan_10_0_0` collision domain shared with router10's eth2 at 10.0.0.2/24) is **administratively shut down**. Its kernel flags are `<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>` — the `UP` bit is absent — and vtysh confirms "eth3 is down / OSPF not enabled on this interface." Because OSPF cannot send or receive Hello packets on a downed interface, the adjacency between router7 and router10 (router-ID 192.168.144.1) was never re-established, which is exactly what router7 logged.

Without the router7–router10 link in OSPF, Campus 1 traffic (entering via router16→router7) can no longer take the direct path through router10 to router17/Campus 2. It is instead detoured via router8 (router7→eth5→router8→router17) or via the WAN (router7→router1→router11→router17), both of which are longer hop paths, producing the observed latency increase with no full outage.

Root cause: router7's eth3 interface was administratively brought down (`ip link set eth3 down`), silently breaking the OSPF adjacency with router10 while leaving ospfd itself intact.

Corrective fix: On router7, run `ip link set eth3 up`. This will restore carrier on the 10.0.0.0/24 segment, OSPF will re-activate the interface, Hello exchange with router10 (10.0.0.2) will proceed, and the Full adjacency will reform within the Dead Interval, restoring the direct Campus 1→router10→Campus 2 path. Restarting ospfd (the team's proposed fix) would accomplish nothing because eth3 would remain admin-down after the restart.
=== END ===